<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Tartu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1632">1632</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as University of Dorpat, Academia Gustaviana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> is the oldest and most prestigious university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, founded in 1632 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Reopened in 1802 under Russian rule as a German-language university, it trained scholars from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. In 1919 it became the national university of independent Estonia, teaching in Estonian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-tartu.jpg" alt="University of Tartu" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: University of Tartu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:University_of_Tartu_circular_logo_in_EST_and_ENG.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> signed the foundation act of the new university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>, now Tartu, on 30 June 1632, while leading a military campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, a few months before his death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a>. The university was called Academia Dorpatensis after its location or Academia Gustaviana after its founder, and its first chancellor was Baron Johan Skytte, governor-general of the Swedish provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>. It was the third oldest university in the Kingdom of Sweden, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> grammar school founded by Stefan Batory in 1583 had existed until 1601. The first students matriculated in April 1632, and the opening ceremony took place on 15 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>It had philosophy, law, theology and medical faculties with the privileges of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>. Because of the Russo-Swedish war of 1656 to 1658 it moved to Tallinn, closed in 1665 and reopened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> in 1690 as Academia Gustavo-Carolina. It then moved to Pärnu in 1699 and was closed after the surrender of that city in 1710 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>.</p><p>The university was relaunched by Baltic German nobles and officially opened in April 1802, with a charter confirmed by Tsar Alexander I, as the first German-speaking university in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Georg Friedrich Parrot was a prominent organiser and its rector. Over half of the professors came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. German was the language of instruction until 1893, and russification between 1882 and 1898 drove out virtually all distinguished German scholars; in 1898 the town and university were renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Yuryev</a>. In 1918 Russian staff and students took refuge in Voronezh, which led to the founding of Voronezh State University.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> became independent in 1918, the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> was an Estonian-language institution from 1919. It was renamed Tartu State University in 1940 to 1941 and again from 1944 to 1989 under Soviet occupation, and was called the Ostland-Universität in Dorpat during the German occupation of 1941 to 1944. Estonia regained independence in 1991, and the university's full academic autonomy can be dated to 1992. Lectinology was founded there in 1888 with Peter Hermann Stillmark's thesis on the isolation of ricin. Its historical buildings are included in the European Heritage Label list.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Tartu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Baltic empire reached Livonia, Ingria and Estonia, where Gustav II Adolf founded the <b>University of Dorpat</b> (Tartu) in 1632.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>University of Tartu</b> became an Estonian-language university, and the song festival tradition begun in 1869 remained central to national life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Estonia and Livonia, Swedish rule is associated with the founding of the <b>University of Dorpat</b> (Tartu) in 1632 and with educational and legal reforms whose institutions survived under later Russian governance, while Swedish Pomerania remained in Swedish hands until 1815.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>University of Dorpat</b>, reopened in 1802, became a centre of Baltic German and later Estonian learning, and Riga grew into one of the empire's main industrial cities and ports.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-livonia">Governorate of Livonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Gustav II Adolf founded the <b>University of Dorpat</b> in 1632, and the pastor Ernst Glück translated the Bible into Latvian in 1685-1689.</blockquote><a href="/state/swedish-livonia">Swedish Livonia</a></li></ul></section>
